"At the beginning of Surah al-Hijr, it is stated that the verses are the verses of the Book and a clear Quran. Those who disbelieve will at some point wish that they had been Muslims. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) stated that on the Day of Resurrection, when the people of Hell see the Muslim sinners coming out of Hell, they will say, \"If only we had been Muslims.\"\n\nAllah states that the disbelievers should be left to eat, drink, and indulge in worldly pleasures, and that in the end they will come to know. No community has ever been destroyed without having a decree known before Allah; no nation's appointed term can be advanced, nor can it be delayed.\n\nThe polytheists of Mecca mocked the Prophet Muhammad, calling him 'madman,' and demanded that he bring angels to them. Allah declares that He does not send down the angels except with truth, and that when they are sent down, the disbelievers will not be granted any respite. It is Allah who revealed the Quran and who will protect it. This protection has manifested in the preservation of the Quran from alteration.\n\nMessengers were also sent to the nations before you, and they were mocked. Allah embeds disbelief into the hearts of the guilty in such a way that they do not believe. Even if a gate from heaven were opened for them and they were to ascend through it, they would still say, 'Our eyes have been dazzled; rather, we are a people bewitched.'\n\nAllah created constellations in the heaven and adorned it for the onlookers. He protected the heaven from every stoned devil. If a 'rajim' (stoned) devil attempts to eavesdrop, a clear shooting flame pursues him. A shihab (shooting flame) is a divine projectile used to repel devils.\n\nIt is Allah who created the earth smoothly and placed firm mountains upon it, brought forth various plants, and created means of livelihood both for humans and for creatures whom they cannot sustain. The treasuries of all things are with Him. He sends the winds to pollinate, sends down water from the sky, and keeps it in reservoirs.\n\nIt is Allah alone who gives life and causes death. He knows those among you who wish to advance in Islam and those who wish to lag behind. Your Lord will gather them on the Day of Resurrection; He is All-Wise, All-Knowing.\n\nIn al-Qurtubi's tafsir, it is debated whether the shooting flames (sparks) kill the jinn devils. Ibn Abbas stated that the shooting flames wound, burn, and strike down, but do not kill. Al-Hasan and a group, however, stated that they do kill. The correct view is the first one. As for the flaming columns repelling human devils, it is either metaphorical or pertains to jinn devils, because it is the hidden devils who first attempt to eavesdrop. This analogy is meant to explain that the Quran is like the heaven; just as the heaven is adorned with stars and protected from devils, the Quran is adorned with verses and surahs and protected.\n\nAs for the earth: Allah spread the earth, set firm mountains upon it, and produced everything therein in due measure. There We provided livelihoods for you and for those whom you cannot sustain. The treasury of everything is with Allah; He sends it down according to a determined measure. Worldly wealth comes to an end, but Allah's power never ends.\n\nWe sent the winds as fertilizing agents. 'Lawaqih' means fertilizing or pollinating. This verse is a scientific miracle. Ibn Abbas stated that the winds pollinate trees and clouds. Al-Razi records this explanation but notes that he did not know how trees are pollinated. Yet modern science has revealed that plants have male and female flowers and that the wind pollinates them. By blowing suitably, winds fertilize plants, and thus Allah's grace becomes manifest. Had there been no winds, there would be no pollination, no rain, and no life.\n\nIndeed, it is We who give life and cause death. The wealth of the deceased passes to heirs, but the ultimate inheritor is Us. We know those who have passed before you and those who are yet to come. Those who lead in faith or disbelief and those who fall behind are all within Our knowledge. Your Lord will surely gather them together. So leave the disbelievers to eat, drink, and amuse themselves; your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.\n\nWe created man from sounding clay, from molded mud. 'Salsal' is dried clay that makes a sound, while 'hama' masnun' is molded, altered, smelling mud. There are various interpretations regarding 'masnun': it carries the meanings of altered, rubbed, poured, or shaped into a form. Man was created from this lowly substance. And before that, We created the jinn from the scorching fire (samum). Samum means poisonous or penetrating the pores of the skin.\n\nYour Lord commanded the angels that He was going to create a human being from dried clay, and that when He breathed into him of His spirit, they should fall down in prostration. The angels prostrated, except Iblis. Iblis said that he would not prostrate to one created from dried clay. Allah expelled him and cursed him until the Day of Judgment. Iblis asked for respite to mislead humanity and was granted it. However, he admitted that he could not mislead the sincere servants. Allah declared that Iblis has no authority over His sincere servants. Hell has seven gates; for each gate, a designated group is assigned.\n\nThe righteous will be in gardens and springs. It will be said to them, 'Enter them in peace.' We will remove whatever rancor is in their hearts; as brothers, they will sit facing each other on couches. No fatigue will touch them there, nor will they ever be removed from it.\n\nAllah informs His servants that He is the Most Forgiving, the Most Merciful, but that His punishment is also a most painful punishment. Then it is requested to give news of the guests of Abraham. The angels came to Abraham and greeted him with peace, but Abraham said he felt fearful of them. When the angels gave him good tidings of a knowledgeable son, Abraham was astonished at these glad tidings despite his old age, but the angels told him that they were giving him good tidings in truth and that he should not despair of the mercy of Allah.\n\nAbraham stated that none despairs of the mercy of his Lord except those who are astray. The angels informed him that they were sent to punish a guilty people, that they would save the family of Lot, but that Lot's wife would be destroyed. When Lot saw the angels, he felt dread, but the angels told him that they had brought the punishment about which his people doubted, and instructed him to set out with his family by night. When Lot's people saw the angels, they came with evil intentions; Lot warned them regarding his guests. Despite Lot offering his daughters, the people persisted. Finally, at sunrise, a dreadful blast seized them, their city was turned upside down, and stones of baked clay were rained down upon them. In this event, there are surely lessons for those who reflect.\n\nThen the people of al-Aykah are mentioned; they too were wrongdoers, and retribution was taken against them. The people of al-Hijr also rejected the messengers, turned away from Our verses, and carved out secure homes from the mountains. But a blast seized them in the morning as well, and nothing of what they had earned availed them. Aykah means a densely wooded place; like the people of Midian, they were a community to whom Shu'ayb was sent. As for the people of al-Hijr, they were the people of Thamud who rejected Salih.\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth in truth; the Hour will surely come. The Prophet is commanded to treat the polytheists gently and act graciously toward them. Your Lord is the Supreme Creator, the All-Knowing. Then, with reference to Surah al-Fatihah, it is stated that 'the seven often-repeated verses' and the great Quran have been given. Al-Fatihah consists of seven verses repeated in every prayer and is the essence of the Quran. The Prophet is told not to covet the worldly possessions of the disbelievers, not to grieve over them, and to act mercifully toward the believers.\n\nThe Prophet will say that he is a clear warner. Just as punishment was sent down upon those who divided the Quran (such as the Jews and Christians), they too will suffer punishment. By your Lord, they will all be questioned about what they did. Then, the Prophet is instructed to openly proclaim what he is commanded, to turn away from the polytheists, and that Allah is sufficient against those who mock. The Prophet is commanded, whenever his breast is constricted, to glorify his Lord with praise, to be among those who prostrate, and to worship until death comes. Here, 'yaqin' means death; that is, because death is certain, worship must be continued."